# Bytes

A `Bytes` value is a finite sequence of octets: a key, a signature, a
digest's raw form, the body of a binary file. A `String` cannot hold one,
since a `String` is UTF-8 text. `Bytes` has no literal. `Bytes(text)` is the
UTF-8 encoding of a text, and a value is written as `0x` followed by its
octets in lowercase hexadecimal, two digits each:

```hql
[Bytes("hé"), Bytes("")]
```

```text
[0x68c3a9, 0x]
```

The `0x` text is how a value is printed by the REPL, `str` and `fmt`; it is
not a literal a program can write. `é` is two octets in UTF-8, so `size`
counts three where a `String`'s `size` counts two characters:

```hql
b = Bytes("hé")
[b.size(), "hé".size()]
```

```text
[3, 2]
```

## From hex and base64

`Bytes(text, Encoding)` reads octets written in an encoding of the module
`encoding`: `Hex`, `Base64`, `Base64Url` or `Percent`. The module is not in
the prelude, so a program writes `import encoding`:

```hql
import encoding
[Bytes("68c3a9", Hex), Bytes("aMOp", Base64)]
```

```text
[0x68c3a9, 0x68c3a9]
```

Text that is not valid in the encoding is an evaluation error naming the
encoding and the first invalid position, counted from 0:

```hql
import encoding
Bytes("68g3", Hex)
```

```text
execute failure[invalid-argument]: the text is not valid Hex: the first invalid character is at position 2
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | Bytes("68g3", Hex)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

`to_bytes` reads the same text as an `Option`: the octets where the text is
valid, and `none` where it is not. It is the form for text a program did
not write itself:

```hql
import encoding
["68c3a9".to_bytes(Hex), "zz".to_bytes(Hex)]
```

```text
[0x68c3a9, none]
```

## Positions and slices

`Bytes` implements `Index<Int>` and `Slice`, through the `bytes` module's
`at` and `slice`. `b[i]` is the octet at `i` as an `Int` in `0..255`, or
`none` past the end, and a negative position counts from the end. `b[a:b]`
is a `Bytes`, clamped to the value's size. `+` on two `Bytes` is
concatenation:

```hql
b = Bytes("hé")
[b[0], b[-1], b[3]]
```

```text
[104, 169, none]
```

```hql
b = Bytes("hé")
[b[0:2], b[1:], b + Bytes("!")]
```

```text
[0x68c3, 0xc3a9, 0x68c3a921]
```

The `bytes` module is in the prelude, like `string`, and is a receiver
module: `b.size()`, `bytes.size(b)` and `b | size` call one function.

## Back to text

`to_text` reads the octets as UTF-8, and gives `none` where they are not
valid UTF-8. `encode` writes them in an encoding, which every octet has:

```hql
import encoding
[Bytes("hé").to_text(), Bytes("ff", Hex).to_text()]
```

```text
["hé", none]
```

```hql
import encoding
b = Bytes("hé")
[b.encode(Hex), b.encode(Base64)]
```

```text
["68c3a9", "aMOp"]
```

For every value `b` and every encoding `E`, `b.encode(E).to_bytes(E)` is
`some(b)`.

## Order and equality

`Bytes` is `Orderable`. Two values compare octet by octet as unsigned
numbers, and a value that is a prefix of another comes first:

```hql
import encoding
[Bytes("ff", Hex), Bytes("0000", Hex), Bytes("01", Hex), Bytes("00", Hex)] | sort
```

```text
[0x00, 0x0000, 0x01, 0xff]
```

Two values are equal when they hold the same octets, however each was
built:

```hql
import encoding
Bytes("hé") == Bytes("68c3a9", Hex)
```

```text
true
```

## Bytes in data

Every text format, JSON, JSON Lines, YAML and CSV, writes a `Bytes` value as
its base64 text, padded, in the standard alphabet:

<!-- run: --to json -->
```hql
import encoding
{name: "key", body: Bytes("ff00", Hex)}
```

```text
{
  "body": "/wA=",
  "name": "key"
}
```

Reading is the other way round only where a type says so. A base64 string
read as `Data` stays a `String`; a field declared `Bytes` reads its text as
base64, and text that is not base64 fails the read:

```hql
type Blob {
    name : String
    body : Bytes
}
Data("{\"name\": \"key\", \"body\": \"aGk=\"}", Json) | Blob
```

```text
Blob(name = "key", body = 0x6869)
```

The digest of a `Bytes` value, and the other text encodings, are in
[[30-digests-and-encodings|Digests and encodings]]. The
[reference](../../reference/std/bytes/index.md) states each function of the
`bytes` module.
